==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 07 NOTES — logrotate
Date: 21-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- logrotate configs live in /etc/logrotate.d/, one per service, read by /etc/logrotate.conf's defaults
- Key directives: daily/weekly/size (when), rotate N (how many copies to keep), compress (save space)
- -d is a safe DRY RUN that shows what would happen without changing anything
- -f FORCES rotation right now, ignoring the normal schedule
- copytruncate copies the log then truncates the ORIGINAL to zero - the process's open file handle
  stays valid (same inode), so it keeps writing normally with no restart/signal needed
- This directly solves the Linux journal Day 02 lsof problem: a plain rename/delete would leave
  a running process writing into an invisible, unreachable old file, wasting disk space forever

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] reading a real /etc/logrotate.d/ config
[ ] logrotate -d (dry run) on an existing config
[ ] writing my own test config with size/rotate/compress/copytruncate
[ ] logrotate -f to force an immediate rotation
[ ] testing copytruncate against a log a background process was actively still writing to

paste or describe what you saw :
